2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - **28BYJ-48 unipolar stepper** (64:1 gearbox), 4096 half-steps/rev or 2048 full-steps/rev; driven by **ULN2003** (4 Darlington channels, 4 input LEDs labeled A/B/C/D = IN1/IN2/IN3/IN4); needs 5 V ≥2 A + **common ground** with the ESP32.
   - **Stepper drive theory**: half-step (8-phase, 1–2 coils on) vs full-step (4-phase, 2 coils always on, ~2× torque); pull-in torque limits start speed; "vibrate in place with holding torque" = either stall (too fast) OR wrong coil-order topology OR a dead coil.
   - **4-wire stepper has exactly 3 distinct coil-order topologies** (necklaces of 4). If straight (1-2-3-4) buzzes, the motor is one of the other two: {IN1,IN2,IN4,IN3} or {IN1,IN3,IN2,IN4}. If NONE of the three rotate → not a wiring-order problem → dead coil / mechanical jam.
   - **ULN2003 input LEDs are on the INPUT side (before the coil)** → a dead motor winding is INVISIBLE to the LEDs (all 4 LEDs can light while a coil is open).
   - **GPIO44 (=U0RXD) re-mux**: `board_feeder_init()` runs early in bringup (before UART0 init) so GPIO44 works then; UART0 initializes LATER and re-muxes GPIO44 back to UART RX (idles HIGH) → clamps IN4 solid. Fix: re-assert `esp32s3_configgpio(pin, OUTPUT)` right before each dispense to reclaim the pin.
